        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 770,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 818,
            "height": 916,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "mgl_surface joins a grid of points into a smooth surface.\n\nWHAT IT TAKES:\n'points' takes a rows by columns by 3 array of positions: neighbours in the grid are joined, two triangles to each square. It needs at least two rows and two columns. An n by 3 array is taken as a single row, and so draws nothing. A cloud frame from the point cloud nodes works too, using its 'point_cloud' entry.\n\nIt is a grid of 3D points, not a list of heights: to draw a height field, build the x and z of each grid point and put the height in y.\n\nPoints can arrive as often as you like and on any thread; the surface is rebuilt on the next render.\n\nHOW IT LOOKS:\nThe surface is lit on both sides, with normals averaged at each point so it shades smoothly. It is an mgl shape: in the current colour and material. An image sent to 'texture' is stretched once across the whole grid.\n\nSYNTAX:\nmgl_surface\n\nIt takes no arguments.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nmgl_surface\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nmgl chain in:\nThe chain. This triggers the drawing.\n\npoints:\nThe grid, as above.\n\nmode / cull / point_size / round / texture:\nAs for the other mgl shapes: drawn solid, as wireframe or as points;\nwhether back faces are skipped; the size of points in pixels; whether points are round; an image mapped onto the surface.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nmgl chain out:\nThe chain, continuing.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.grid makes a flat grid of points to start from. mgl_mesh draws triangles of any arrangement; mgl_plane is a flat subdivided rectangle.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 810,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 900,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "mgl_surface joins a grid of points into a smooth surface.\n\nWHAT IT TAKES:\n'points' takes a rows by columns by 3 array of positions: neighbours in the grid are joined, two triangles to each square. It needs at least two rows and two columns. An n by 3 array is taken as a single row, and so draws nothing. A cloud frame from the point cloud nodes works too, using its 'point_cloud' entry.\n\nIt is a grid of 3D points, not a list of heights: to draw a height field, build the x and z of each grid point and put the height in y.\n\nPoints can arrive as often as you like and on any thread; the surface is rebuilt on the next render.\n\nHOW IT LOOKS:\nThe surface is lit on both sides, with normals averaged at each point so it shades smoothly. It is an mgl shape: in the current colour and material. An image sent to 'texture' is stretched once across the whole grid.\n\nSYNTAX:\nmgl_surface\n\nIt takes no arguments.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nmgl_surface\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nmgl chain in:\nThe chain. This triggers the drawing.\n\npoints:\nThe grid, as above.\n\nmode / cull / point_size / round / texture:\nAs for the other mgl shapes: drawn solid, as wireframe or as points;\nwhether back faces are skipped; the size of points in pixels; whether points are round; an image mapped onto the surface.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nmgl chain out:\nThe chain, continuing.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.grid makes a flat grid of points to start from. mgl_mesh draws triangles of any arrangement; mgl_plane is a flat subdivided rectangle."
        },
